> Derek Ray wrote:
>> On 2007-11-27, Henry J Cobb <hcobb@io.com> wrote:
>>> That cap should apply to all classes, but I'd rather have dragon armor 
>>> count as metal armor, except for a narrow range of spells.
>> It's not intended to apply to all classes.  It's intended to be a body
>> armor restriction on wizards; not just DSM, but _all_ armor, including
>> leather.  Working as designed.
>
> For once I have to agree with Henry: why is a wizard's spellcasting 
> restricted in a way that no other class's spellcasting is?  If 
> spellcasting should be restricted in wizards who wear armor, why is it 
> not restricted in nonwizards who wear armor?

Why are monks restricted in to-hit by wearing body armor, and no other
class is?

It is assumed that all other classes have learned to compensate to some
degree for wearing body armor while casting; as such, their capacity and
diversity in spells they are capable of casting has suffered greatly in
comparison to wizards, who have the capacity to advance in _every spell
school_ (and typically to advance further, though "clerical" is an
exception here).

> I admit there is /some/ precedent for this in RPG systems. 

There's a great deal of precedent for it, actually, in more than just a
few ways.  Even basic D&D had the restriction, though it was mostly just
"the wizard can't even put the stuff on".  I'm a bit more permissive;
you can put it on, but it'll take away part of the thing that makes you
a wizard.

> But when the same spell is cast by two characters with the same Int, 
> Wis, XL, skill and armor, why is the nonwizard better?

See above.  The wizard should take the armor off; at that point he'd be
able to cast with the same success ratio at a noticeably lower Int, Wis,
XL, and skill level.

> Also, do monks get the same body-armor penalty for spellcasting?

No.  Monks already get a body-armor penalty related to their own
intended specialty, unarmed combat.  (Though it applies to when he's
wielding a weapon too, the implication here is that he's spent all his
time learning to fight unarmed and bare-bodies, so he loses some of that
when he puts body armor on.)

>> Then the wizard should take the armor off, shouldn't he?
> but the Rogue gets the spells for free?

Rogues can advance the following spell schools:

Divination, Escape, and Matter to Skilled.

Wizards can advance the following:

Attack, Healing, Divination, Escape, Matter, and Enchantment to Expert;
Clerical to Basic.

The Rogue doesn't get the spells for free; for the ability to wear and
fight in body armor, as well as the ability to backstab fleeing
monsters, he gave up "Expert" status in his three schools as well as any
skill at all in four others.

This will matter even more when "Expert" level in a spell becomes more
than just lip service to the casting percentages; right now, most spells
do not scale much, if at all with skill ("attack" being a big exception), 
and I fully intend for as many of them to do so as appropriate.

